//! check: fail(E1001)
//! phase: typecheck
//! conforms: std.fs, mem.tier0.move.1, mem.tier0.move.2
//!
//! **The staleness discipline, held as a rejection.** `fs.close` takes
//! its `File` by `take` (X1), so reading a closed file is a use-after-move
//! — caught statically, with no generation counter, no liveness bit and no
//! run-time cost. This file is the witness: it is expected to be REJECTED,
//! and if a future pin ever accepts it the rig goes red, which is exactly
//! what a discipline that lives in the type system should do.
//!
//! It is also the argument for `take` over a `mut`-and-flag design: a
//! `File` with an `is_open` field would move this error from compile time
//! to run time and add a row to every operation for a condition the
//! compiler can see.

use std.fs

fn main() -> !int {
    let p = "use-after-close.tmp"
    var f = fs.create(p)?
    fs.close(take f)?
    let after = fs.read(mut f, 4) else "?"
    print("unreachable: {after}")
    0
}
